WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.920
Filmmakers today can bring just about anything
to life using state of the art digital effects.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.530
Whether it's exploding space stations or giant
transforming robots, if you can imagine it,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:14.540
the movie industry can spare a couple hundred
million to put it up on the big screen.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.860
But all that technology can also be used for
decidedly more mundane purposes - like making

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.580
an actor's body parts look bigger or erasing
embarrassing tattoos.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.800
In fact, the movie industry's top effects
animators are often hard at work adding and

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.500
subtracting tiny elements that may never be
noticed by most filmgoers.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.590
Here's a look at some actors whose real-life
features had to be digitally altered for the

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:38.829
movies.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:40.799
Dakota Johnson

00:00:40.799 --> 00:00:45.690
Whenever you see a new d scene in a movie,
there's a good chance that nobody on set ever

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.079
really got nekid.

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:51.949
Often, actors wear flesh-colored underwear,
and for most movies this is enough, because

00:00:51.949 --> 00:00:54.979
intimate scenes don't really factor into the
plot.

00:00:54.979 --> 00:00:58.339
Not true for the film adaptation of Fifty
Shades of Grey.

00:00:58.339 --> 00:01:03.440
Those scenes are the plot, which posed a unique
problem for the effects department.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.289
Specifically because it showed more skin than
most other films do, a decision needed to

00:01:07.289 --> 00:01:12.110
be made about whether or not Dakota Johnson's
character, Anastasia Steele, had a personal

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:14.079
grooming routine.

00:01:14.079 --> 00:01:18.609
Apparently she didn't, because a digital effects
artist had to go in during post-production

00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:22.820
and use CGI to give Johnson, um, one of THESE.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.350
Cinematographer Seamus McGarvey would later
refer to this as one of the most surreal moments

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.400
of his entire career - closely followed by
overseeing the hiring process of a suitable

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.560
butt double for Johnson.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.070
Nicolas Cage

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.299
It's safe to say that Nicolas Cage loves comic
books.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:44.799
His son Kal-El is named after Superman, he
changed his own last name early in his career

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:49.710
from Coppola to Cage as a nod to the Marvel
character Luke Cage, and he even has a large

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.259
tattoo of the Ghost Rider on his arm.

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:57.079
This latter tribute proved to be a problem
when Cage was set to star in the 2007 Ghost

00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:03.280
Rider film, because, well, it would have been
kind of weird for Johnny Blaze to have a tattoo

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.430
of his own superhero alter ego on his bicep.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:11.400
So the decision was made to use the magic
of special effects to hide the offending tattoo

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.690
whenever Cage needed to take off his shirt.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.930
A persistent rumor surrounding the movie suggests
that Cage's abs were also created using the

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:24.600
magic of CGI; however, his Ghost Rider co-star
Eva Mendes has confirmed that they were, in

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.200
fact, 100 percent real.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.870
Kristian Nairn

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.540
There's a scene in the first season of Game
of Thrones when the character Hodor, played

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:47.230
by actor Kristian Nairn, gets nekid - revealing
the giant character's suitably plus-sized

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.230
anatomy.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:53.510
To achieve the illusion, Nairn was asked to
wear a realistic-looking 16-inch prosthetic

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.250
that was attached to his groin using glue.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:04.370
"For weeks later, I would be finding pieces
of prosthetic piece, um, attached to my own."

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:09.659
The effects department then blended the whole
thing to his body using digital effects, airbrushing

00:03:09.659 --> 00:03:13.010
out a special thong Nairn was wearing beneath
the whole get-up.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:17.290
According to the actor, this thong shielded
his actual package from view so well that

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.430
one of his co-stars asked if it was real.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:23.330
There's only one response to that question.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.409
"Hodor."

00:03:25.409 --> 00:03:26.849
Paul Reubens

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:30.640
If you happened to catch Pee-Wee's Big Holiday
on Netflix, you may have noticed that actor

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.620
Paul Reubens looked surprisingly spry and
youthful for a 60-something-year-old man.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:40.720
To give the impression that Reubens hadn't
aged since his last TV appearance as the character,

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.760
CGI was used in tandem with makeup, lighting
and sticky tape to de-age the actor.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.160
And that last part isn't a joke - tape was
used to pull back Reubens' face for some scenes

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:52.859
to make his skin look smoother.

00:03:52.859 --> 00:03:55.799
Sometimes, the simplest solutions are the
best ones.

00:03:55.799 --> 00:04:01.000
To his credit, Reubens was surprisingly open
about the use of CGI to remove his wrinkles,

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.000
admitting that Pee-Wee simply wouldn't work
"with age mixed into it."

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.220
"Breakfast bar!

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:08.940
Scone!

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:10.080
French toast!

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:10.980
American toast!

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:11.989
Achoo!"

00:04:11.989 --> 00:04:13.750
The cast of Glee

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.040
The hit musical dramedy Glee continued the
longstanding Hollywood tradition of hiring

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.040
older actors to play teenagers.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.570
Although none of the actors playing high schoolers
on the show were the age they were supposed

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:27.540
to be, they still apparently suffered from
a fairly common affliction that plagues all

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.720
young people: acne.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.350
This didn't zit well with the producers, who
paid an unnamed visual effects company to

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:39.690
do what was dubbed "a pimple pass" on most
episodes to ensure every actor's skin was

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:41.190
blemish-free.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.480
Because if there's anything that's going to
make a show relatable to young people, it's

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:50.090
flawless-looking actors with perfect skin
complaining about being unattractive.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.290
Jessica Alba

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.610
Jessica Alba resolved long ago to not take
off her clothes for a movie.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.650
She told Scarlet magazine in 2010:

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.160
"I can act sexy and wear sexy clothes, but
I can't go naked.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.631
My grandmother would freak out and throw a
towel over me if she saw me wearing just a

00:05:05.631 --> 00:05:09.970
bra and panties…I can handle being sexy
with clothes on but not with them off."

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:14.720
Anyone who watched 2010's Machete after reading
the article in Scarlet was likely surprised

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.940
when Alba appeared to be newd during a shower
scene - but the key word there is "appeared."

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:24.770
In reality, Alba was clothed, and her garments
were digitally replaced in post-production

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:27.540
with computer-generated skin.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.540
Claire Danes

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:33.800
When a regular for a hit show gets pregnant,
sometimes creators use the tools the universe

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.960
has given them and write the pregnancy into
the story.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.400
But when Claire Danes announced she was pregnant
with her first child in 2012, she assured

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:46.201
fans her Homeland character Carrie Mathison
would remain "fervently non-pregnant" for

00:05:46.201 --> 00:05:48.190
the show's second season.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:52.740
Danes' on-set work for season two of Homeland
continued as late as six weeks before she

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:53.740
gave birth.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:59.130
Her baby bump was digitally erased in post-production,
and body doubles were also used to help with

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:00.130
the subterfuge.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:03.800
It wouldn't be the last time Homeland used
these strategies to keep a pregnancy out of

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:08.720
the story - the following year, they used
similar techniques on Morena Baccarin.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.060
Even though it didn't show up on-screen, Danes'
pregnancy didn't make things any easier for

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:14.060
her.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.160
She described one particularly difficult scene
that had her chained in a basement.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:18.960
"It was 4 a.m.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:25.990
I was seven and a half months pregnant, and
I was like, 'This sucks.'"

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:30.180
She also said the pregnancy made love scenes
difficult, using the example of the shooting

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.940
of one love scene when her baby was particularly
active.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.720
"It was like he was protesting on my husband's
behalf."

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:38.200
Chris Evans

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:43.060
Among all the otherworldly characters in Marvel
movies, the young, scrawny Steve Rogers in

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.770
2011's Captain America: The First Avenger
remains one of Marvel's most impressive CGI

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:48.800
effects.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:53.470
No single technique was used to create the
smaller and skinnier pre-experiment Steve

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:54.660
Rogers.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:58.810
Marvel hired Lola Visual Effects to do the
digital body work on Chris Evans for Captain

00:06:58.810 --> 00:06:59.810
America.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:04.270
Lola's supervisor Edson Williams told Variety
that the so-called "Benjamin Button" method

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:09.510
- or digitally attaching Evans' face to a
body double - was only used for 15 percent

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:10.610
of the shots.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.290
For the rest, Lola digitally shrunk Evans'
face and body.

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:19.190
The process involved shooting everything at
least three times: Once with Evans, once with

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:23.590
Evans' smaller body double, and one "clean
shot" without either of them.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:27.960
With each shot, the team determined whether
to go the easier "Benjamin Button" route,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.010
or what Lola came to call "Steve slimming."

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:35.050
Williams said that the most challenging part
of the process was caused by Evans' massive

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:36.050
arms.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:40.160
When shot in profile, they blocked an entire
third of his body, so the fabric of Evans'

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:44.680
shirts would need to be digitally erased and
replaced with something else to give the illusion

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:47.810
of skinny and comparatively weak arms.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:51.580
That's probably the only time in the history
of superhero movies that bulking up turned

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.620
out to be a bad thing.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:56.060
Guy Henry &amp; Ingvild Deila

00:07:56.060 --> 00:08:01.120
For better or worse, prequels are now a staple
of film franchises, and CGI is part of what

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:02.470
makes them possible.

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:06.340
The filmmakers of Rogue One faced particularly
tough challenges.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:10.680
For the story they wanted, they needed to
recreate the characters of Grand Moff Tarkin

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.220
and Princess Leia.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:18.140
Peter Cushing, who played Tarkin in 1977's
Star Wars, passed away in 1994.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:22.080
Carrie Fisher, who portrayed Princess Leia,
was still alive during the making of Rogue

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.660
One, but she looked and sounded nothing like
she had...since about four decades had passed.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.880
So Tarkin and Leia were digitally recreated.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.289
For principal photography, Tarkin was played
by British actor Guy Henry, who wore a motion-capture

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:38.159
headpiece during filming.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:41.729
Animators worked meticulously to recreate
the actor's mannerisms.

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:45.800
Not quite so much work was needed for the
digital rebirth of Princess Leia.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:49.730
When seen from behind, she's fully Norwegian
actress Ingvild Deila.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:55.180
From the front, what you see is almost entirely
a digital creation, though Leia's outstretched

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:57.139
hand is Deila's.

00:08:57.139 --> 00:08:58.970
Patrick Stewart &amp; Ian McKellen

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:03.939
X-Men: The Last Stand is not a well-loved
movie among comic book fans, but at least

00:09:03.939 --> 00:09:06.759
one aspect the film was pretty groundbreaking.

00:09:06.759 --> 00:09:11.630
The film opens with Professor X and Magneto
making an early attempt to recruit Jean Grey

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.889
into Xavier's school for mutants.

00:09:13.889 --> 00:09:17.470
The flashback sequence takes place around
20 years before the events of the rest of

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:21.970
the film, and the filmmakers wanted the actors'
appearance to reflect those younger days.

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:27.180
Rather than using prosthetics or special motion
capture suits, the sequence was shot as if

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:29.720
no changes were going to be made to it at
all.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:36.180
The VFX team then used a process called digital
skin grafting to rejuvenate Stewart and McKellen,

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:40.259
utilizing old photographs for reference as
well as consulting with a plastic surgeon

00:09:40.259 --> 00:09:44.269
to learn the specifics of how skin changes
as people age.

00:09:44.269 --> 00:09:45.750
Kurt Russell

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:49.870
Like the film that preceded it, Guardians
of the Galaxy Volume 2 opens on Earth, several

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.600
decades in the past.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.420
We see Meredith Quill being romanced by a
Kurt Russell who looks like he could've just

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:58.889
finished filming Big Trouble in Little China.

00:09:58.889 --> 00:10:03.759
But there seems to be a little disagreement
regarding how his onscreen rejuvenation was

00:10:03.759 --> 00:10:04.759
achieved.

00:10:04.759 --> 00:10:08.860
When Russell talked about the transformation,
he said it was "about 90%" make-up and added

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:12.520
that the filmmakers didn't do much digital
manipulation.

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:16.910
Russell says he's been working with the same
make-up artist - Dennis Ledger - since 1989,

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:18.930
and Ledger pulled off most of the work.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.870
"They just did the little brush up, clean
up on a couple of things, but it's pretty

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:24.209
much what Dennis Ledger did."

00:10:24.209 --> 00:10:28.079
Writer-slash-director James Gunn's recollection
is significantly different.

00:10:28.079 --> 00:10:32.560
In the comments section of a video Gunn posted
on Facebook, the director responded to a fan

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.259
question about the flashback sequence.

00:10:34.259 --> 00:10:35.259
He wrote:

00:10:35.259 --> 00:10:39.620
"A company named Lola did the effects, and
they did an incredible job.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:41.649
First we film every scene with Kurt.

00:10:41.649 --> 00:10:46.000
A young actor, Aaron Schwarz […] watches
everything [Kurt Russell] does.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.769
He then goes in and mimics Kurt's actions.

00:10:48.769 --> 00:10:53.860
We then take Kurt's acting and general face
and body and place Aaron's skin onto him.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:58.860
It is a long, painstaking process that took
many, many months to accomplish."

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:00.600
Robert Downey Jr.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:05.671
For all the CGI used in Captain America: Civil
War, the film's most stunning visual effect

00:11:05.671 --> 00:11:08.100
came in a comparatively low-key moment.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.079
An early scene in Civil War opens to what
appears to be a flashback.

00:11:12.079 --> 00:11:17.089
A young Tony Stark is giving exactly the snark
we expect from him as his parents Howard and

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:18.579
Maria are about to leave for a fateful trip.

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:22.260
"Who's the homeless person on the couch?"

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:24.580
"This is why I love coming home for Christmas.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:26.180
Right before you leave town."

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.860
We eventually learn we're seeing Stark's memories
as they're translated through a device Stark

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:31.860
has developed.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:37.019
As they did when digitally rejuvenating characters
in earlier films, Marvel Studios called on

00:11:37.019 --> 00:11:41.070
Lola Visual Effects to take 20 to 25 years
off Downey.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:45.249
The scene presented one of the toughest challenges
Lola has had to tackle while working with

00:11:45.249 --> 00:11:48.790
Marvel because the entire sequence was filmed
in a single shot.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:53.329
Trent Claus, Lola's visual effects supervisor,
told The Hollywood Reporter: "The shot was

00:11:53.329 --> 00:11:59.170
nearly 4,000 frames long, with Tony Stark
turning from one side to the other multiple

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:04.370
times, physically interacting with other actors,
and the set itself, and moving closer to the

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:07.720
camera for a very long, uninterrupted close-up."

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:12.829
Claus went on to say that analyzing footage
from Downey's late '80s film work was essential

00:12:12.829 --> 00:12:17.730
in achieving the effect, and singled out 1987's
Less Than Zero as a focal point.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:20.189
"But it's so real, it freaked me out."

00:12:20.189 --> 00:12:21.189
"You think?

00:12:21.189 --> 00:12:22.189
It freaked me the heck out!

00:12:22.189 --> 00:12:26.720
I was like, 'My God, he was beautiful, what
happened?'"

